  "image_name": "7f5a497d658c6691890dc924765b6ef1.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure illustrates how layered sodium-containing transition-metal materials change during battery charging and repeated cycling. In the intact, “pristine” material, Na⁺ ions sit between ordered transition-metal layers. When the material is charged and sodium is extracted, the transition-metal layers shift relative to each other, shown as a gliding motion. This suggests that removing Na⁺ destabilizes the original layered arrangement and drives a structural rearrangement.\n\nThe lower part emphasizes what happens when the material is already strained. Starting from a similarly layered structure, charging again removes Na⁺, but now the lattice develops visible defects, including dislocations and stacking faults. With continued cycling—repeated sodium insertion and extraction—these defects accumulate and the layered particles break apart, leading to fragmentation and cracking.\n\nOverall, the figure conveys a degradation pathway in layered Na-ion electrode materials: sodium extraction causes layer gliding, strain promotes defect formation, and repeated cycling amplifies these structural disruptions until the material mechanically fails. The main message is a link between electrochemical cycling, atomic-scale layer motion, defect generation, and eventual cracking of the electrode.",
